I can provide you with an outline and some code snippets to help you get started on creating a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current webpage.

1. Create a new folder for your extension.
2. Inside this folder, create two files: `manifest.json` and `content.js`.
3. In `manifest.json`, add the following content:

```json
{
  "name": "Save Page Contents",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the contents of the current page.",
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "storage"],
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"]
  },
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content.js"]
    }
  ]
}
```

4. Add an icon file named `icon.png` in the same directory as `manifest.json`. This will be used as the browser action's icon.
5. Create another file called `popup.html` inside the same directory. Add the following HTML code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Save Page Contents</title>
  <style>
    body {
      width: 200px;
      padding: 10px;
    }
    button {
      width: 100%;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Save Page Contents</h1>
  <button id="saveButton">Save</button>

  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

6. Create a file called `popup.js` in the same directory. Add the following JavaScript code:

```javascript
document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, function(tabs) {
    chrome.tabs.sendMessage(tabs[0].id, { action: 'savePage' });
  });
});
```

7. Finally, create a file called `background.js` in the same directory. Add the following JavaScript code:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
  if (request.action === 'savePage') {
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(sender.tab.id, { file: 'content.js' });
  }
});
```

Now, let's move on to `content.js`. This script will run on every webpage and handle saving its contents when requested by the popup.

Create a file called `content.js` in the same directory. Add the following JavaScript code:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
  if (request.action === 'savePage') {
    const pageTitle = document.title;
    const pageContent = document.body.innerHTML;

    // Save the data using local storage or any other method you prefer
    localStorage.setItem(pageTitle, pageContent);

    console.log(`Saved ${pageTitle} successfully!`);
  }
});
```

With these steps completed, you should have a basic Chrome extension that allows users to save the contents of the current webpage. To test it out, load the extension into Chrome by going to `chrome://extensions/`, enabling Developer Mode, and clicking "Load unpacked". Select the folder containing your extension files.

Please note that this is just a simple example, and there are many ways to improve upon it, such as adding error handling, allowing users to choose where to store their saved pages, etc. Additionally, keep in mind that storing large amounts of data in Local Storage may not be ideal due to size limitations and potential performance issues. Consider alternative methods like IndexedDB or server-side storage solutions depending on your specific use case.

