Here is a JavaScript function that calculates the nth Fibonacci number modulo p using an efficient approach called matrix exponentiation:

```javascript
function fibonacciModulo(n, p) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;

  const baseMatrix = [[1, 1], [1, 0]];
  let resultMatrix = power(baseMatrix, n - 1);

  return resultMatrix[0][0] % p;
}

function multiplyMatrices(a, b) {
  const c = [
    [a[0][0] * b[0][0] + a[0][1] * b[1][0], a[0][0] * b[0][1] + a[0][1] * b[1][1]],
    [a[1][0] * b[0][0] + a[1][1] * b[1][0], a[1][0] * b[0][1] + a[1][1] * b[1][1]]
  ];

  for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
      c[i][j] %= p;
    }
  }

  return c;
}

function power(matrix, exponent) {
  if (exponent === 1) return matrix;

  let halfPower = power(matrix, Math.floor(exponent / 2));
  let squaredHalfPower = multiplyMatrices(halfPower, halfPower);

  if (exponent % 2 === 0) {
    return squaredHalfPower;
  } else {
    return multiplyMatrices(squaredHalfPower, matrix);
  }
}
```

This code defines two helper functions: `multiplyMatrices` to perform matrix multiplication with modulo operation, and `power` to calculate the matrix raised to a given exponent using binary exponentiation.

The main function `fibonacciModulo` uses these helpers to compute the desired result efficiently.
