Here's a simple example of how you might write a Metal compute kernel for Gaussian blurring an image:

```metal
#include <metal_stdlib>
using namespace metal;

// Define the size of the Gaussian kernel (must be odd)
const int KERNEL_SIZE = 5;
const float SIGMA = 1.0f; // Standard deviation

// Function to calculate the Gaussian weight for each pixel in the kernel
float gaussianWeight(int x, int y) {
    float dx = x - KERNEL_SIZE / 2;
    float dy = y - KERNEL_SIZE / 2;
    return exp(-(dx * dx + dy * dy) / (2 * SIGMA * SIGMA)) / (2 * M_PI * SIGMA * SIGMA);
}

kernel void gaussianBlur(texture2d<float, access::read_write> inputTexture [[texture(0)]],
                         texture2d<float, access::write> outputTexture [[texture(1)]],
                         uint2 gid [[thread_position_in_grid]]) {

    // Get the current pixel position
    uint2 pos = gid.xy;

    // Initialize variables to hold the sum and count of weighted pixels
    float4 sum = float4(0.0f);
    float count = 0.0f;

    // Iterate over the neighborhood defined by the kernel size
    for (int i = -KERNEL_SIZE / 2; i <= KERNEL_SIZE / 2; ++i) {
        for (int j = -KERNEL_SIZE / 2; j <= KERNEL_SIZE / 2; ++j) {
            // Calculate the new position within the kernel
            uint2 newPos = pos + uint2(i, j);

            // Check if the new position is within bounds
            if (newPos.x >= 0 && newPos.x < inputTexture.get_width() &&
                newPos.y >= 0 && newPos.y < inputTexture.get_height()) {

                // Get the color at the new position
                float4 color = inputTexture.read(newPos);

                // Calculate the weight based on the distance from center
                float weight = gaussianWeight(i, j);

                // Add the weighted color to the sum
                sum += color * weight;

                // Increment the count
                count += weight;
            }
        }
    }

    // Write the blurred result back to the output texture
    outputTexture.write(sum / count, pos);
}
```

This code defines a basic Gaussian blur operation using a 5x5 kernel with a standard deviation (`SIGMA`) of 1.0. The `gaussianWeight` function calculates the weight for each pixel in the kernel based on its distance from the center.

The main kernel function iterates over the neighborhood defined by the kernel size, reads the colors from the input texture, applies the weights, sums them up, and writes the resulting blurred color to the output texture.

Please note that this implementation assumes that the input and output textures have the same dimensions. If they don't, additional checks or adjustments would be needed to handle edge cases properly.
