To prevent XPath injection, you should avoid directly concatenating user input into your XPath expressions. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements if possible. However, since Java's standard libraries do not support parameterized XPath queries natively, we can sanitize the inputs by escaping special characters.

Here’s how you can modify your code to make it less vulnerable:

```java
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;
import org.xml.sax.InputSource;
import java.io.StringReader;

public class SafeXPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Sample XML data
            String xmlData = "<users><user name='john' pass='password123'/></users>";

            // User inputs (these would normally come from somewhere else)
            String user = "john";
            String pass = "password123";

            // Escape special characters in the user input
            user = escapeXml(user);
            pass = escapeXml(pass);

            // Construct the safe XPath expression
            String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + user + "' and @pass='" + pass + "']";

            // Parse the XML data
            InputSource source = new InputSource(new StringReader(xmlData));
            XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();
            NodeList nodeList = (NodeList) xPath.compile(expression).evaluate(source, XPathConstants.NODESET);

            // Check if any nodes were found
            if (nodeList.getLength() > 0) {
                System.out.println("User found!");
            } else {
                System.out.println("No user found.");
            }
        } catch (XPathExpressionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private static String escapeXml(String input) {
        return input.replace("&", "&amp;")
                    .replace("<", "&lt;")
                    .replace(">", "&gt;")
                    .replace("\"", "&quot;")
                    .replace("'", "&apos;");
    }
}
```

In this example:
- The `escapeXml` method escapes common XML special characters that could be used for injection.
- The constructed XPath expression uses these escaped values instead of raw user input.

This approach helps mitigate some risks but does not completely eliminate all potential vulnerabilities. For more robust security measures, consider using an external library like OWASP ESAPI or implementing additional validation checks on the server side before constructing the XPath query.

Here's an example of using the Strategy Pattern in JavaScript for a banking application:

```javascript
// Define the interface for payment strategies
class PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount) {}
}

// Concrete strategy: Credit Card Payment
class CreditCardPayment extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(cardNumber, expiryDate, cvv) {
    super();
    this.cardNumber = cardNumber;
    this.expiryDate = expiryDate;
    this.cvv = cvv;
  }

  pay(amount) {
    console.log(`Processing credit card payment (${this.cardNumber}) for $${amount}`);
    // Perform actual credit card processing here...
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: PayPal Payment
class PayPalPayment extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(email) {
    super();
    this.email = email;
  }

  pay(amount) {
    console.log(`Processing PayPal payment (${this.email}) for $${amount}`);
    // Perform actual PayPal processing here...
  }
}

// Context class representing the user's account
class BankAccount {
  constructor(balance) {
    this.balance = balance;
  }

  setPaymentStrategy(strategy) {
    this.paymentStrategy = strategy;
  }

  makePayment(amount) {
    if (this.paymentStrategy) {
      const result = this.paymentStrategy.pay(amount);
      if (result === true) {
        this.balance -= amount;
        console.log(`Payment successful! New balance: $${this.balance}`);
      } else {
        console.log('Payment failed.');
      }
    } else {
      console.log('No payment strategy set.');
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const myBankAccount = new BankAccount(1000);

// Set up different payment methods based on user preferences
myBankAccount.setPaymentStrategy(new CreditCardPayment('4111-1111-1111-1111', '12/25', '123'));
myBankAccount.makePayment(50); // Process credit card payment

myBankAccount.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment('john.doe@example.com'));
myBankAccount.makePayment(75); // Process PayPal payment
```

In this example, we have defined two concrete strategies (`CreditCardPayment` and `PayPalPayment`) that implement the `PaymentStrategy` interface. The `BankAccount` context class uses these strategies to process payments.

The key benefit of using the Strategy Pattern is that it allows us to easily switch between different payment methods without modifying the core logic of our banking application. This makes our code more flexible, maintainable, and extensible.
